
## Indexes, access paths and TPROC-C (measured, 1.58)

Every table is stored by rowid.  A composite PRIMARY KEY and every UNIQUE
constraint become an internal UNIQUE index (`sqlite_autoindex_<t>_<n>`,
as SQLite names them); CREATE INDEX takes any number of columns with
ASC/DESC.  Index keys are an order-preserving tuple encoding
(`[0xFE][index id][elements...][rowid]`, xstore.c), so a prefix is a
range.  One access-path chooser (vexec.c) serves SELECT, aggregates,
ORDER BY, UPDATE and DELETE: rowid range, longest-equality-prefix index
seek with an optional range on the next column, index order instead of a
sort, MIN/MAX from the first or last key, and index nested-loop joins.
Statements are parsed and planned once (per-statement plan cache).

TPROC-C runs natively under Turso's perf/tpc-c driver (35 prepared
statements, all seeks; test_tpcc_seek asserts the rows visited) and
HammerDB's quackoltp.tcl.  Write-write conflicts are found at the
statement with row write intents (deadlock detection, bounded wait), so
TPROC-C's retries are cheap.  Size the buffer pool: `--storage-frames`
(see docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md).  Numbers against SQLite and Turso:
docs/examples/sqlxtc.md, "Benchmark".

## SQL-dialect compatibility with SQLite (measured, 1.58)

sqlxtc is a from-scratch engine (no vendored sqlite3.c) speaking the
Quack JSON-over-TCP protocol, so SQLite's own testfixture TCL suite does
NOT apply -- it drives SQLite's C library and asserts SQLite-internal
behavior no independent engine reproduces.  The meaningful measure is
SQL-level result-set equivalence: statements harvested from SQLite's
test corpus (test/sqlxtc/harvest_sqlite_tests.py), run through both
sqlxtc and python's sqlite3, comparing results.

On SQLite 3.53.4's test/*.test: 1171 of 1413 evaluated statements
(82.9%) give SQLite-identical results; v1.57.0 gave 976 of 1248 (78.2%).
The 2 remaining mismatches are `substr()` with hex arguments above 2^32,
where sqlxtc follows SQLite 3.53.4 and the python oracle (SQLite 3.46.1)
differs.  Everything else that does not match is rejected ("unsupported
SQL"), never answered differently.  Still unsupported: NATURAL joins,
USING with ORDER BY on a non-projected column, multi-key or 3+-way
outer joins, window functions, recursive and multiple CTEs, triggers,
virtual tables, ALTER TABLE ADD/DROP COLUMN, JSON.  NOT NULL, CHECK,
DEFAULT and FOREIGN KEY clauses are parsed but not enforced (an INSERT
that SQLite would refuse is accepted) -- the one deliberate gap in the
"rejected, never answered differently" rule above.  Wired-in differential tests: test/sqlxtc/
test_sqlxtc_compat.py (163 cases + 4 expected-unsupported), the oracle
(32), and the TPC-C differential test_sqlxtc_tpcc.py (494 steps).
